len=2.\n\nLooks correct. Let me check my Rust impl of is_string_bytes:\n\nLooking at my code:\n```rust\n} else {\n    // Search for an alternative\n    while len > 0 && p_idx < p.len() {\n        let ch = p[p_idx];\n        p_idx += 1;\n        len -= 1;\n        if ch == 0x0E { break; }\n    }\n    if len <= 0 {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    len -= 1;  // BUG: this should not decrement len if we found the alternative\n    s_idx = start;\n    mlen = 0;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, in the C, after the search loop, `if (len-- <= 0) return 0;` — this is \"if (len <= 0) return 0; len--;\" but with side-effect. Actually `len--` is post-decrement, so the value of `len` is checked first, THEN decremented. So if len was 0, return 0. Otherwise, decrement to len-1.\n\nBut wait - in C the search loop:\n```c\nwhile (len>0 && *p++ != '\\xE') len--;\n```\n\nWhen `*p` is 0xE, the loop exits, but the post-increment still happens (so `p` moves past the 0xE), BUT `len--` does NOT happen because the body is empty and the loop tested condition was false. So len is unchanged from the last iteration.\n\nInitial len=5, p='X'.\n- iter: *p evals to X, true, p++, len-- → len=4, p='B'\n- iter: *p evals to B, true, p++, len-- → len=3, p=0xE\n- iter: *p evals to 0xE, false, p++ (now past 0xE), len NOT decremented → len=3\n\nSo after loop len=3. Then `if (len-- <= 0)` — checks 3<=0 false, decrement → len=2.\n\nIn my Rust code:\n```rust\nwhile len > 0 && p_idx < p.len() {\n    let ch = p[p_idx];\n    p_idx += 1;\n    len -= 1;\n    if ch == 0x0E { break; }\n}\n```\n\nHere I always decrement len, including when ch is 0x0E. That's WRONG.\n\nLet me also check the second `len-- <= 0`. Actually re-examining: in C, that's checking `len <= 0` and decrementing. If len was 0 (no bytes were consumed in inner loop because already 0), return 0. Let's re-fix:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1071,"cache_read_input_tokens":108720,"cache_